Executive Summary:&nbsp;




The majority of Russia’s federal subjects have experienced fuel shortages due to Ukrainian attacks on Russian oil infrastructure. Russian President Vladimir Putin has sought to downplay the severity of the deficits and has delegated crisis management to regional governors in an attempt to shield the Kremlin from public blame.



The Kremlin has prevented governors from looking for available fuel on the open market, creating a system in which governors with connections in influential patronage networks are likely to receive more support from state oil companies than those with lower-level personal connections.



The current fuel deficit will not lead to true open dissent from regional heads, as the Kremlin places more emphasis on the appearance of problem-solving than on good governance, greatly reducing the pressure on governors.&nbsp;



